{"path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment1.md","content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT: this fix's contract is invisible to the repo's own IT suite**

The bug class this line fixes (an ASC-rejected query parameter) is exactly what the IT fake endpoints cannot see: `test/store_appearance_check_test.dart` routes requests by **path only** — `if (path.startsWith('/asc/v1/betaGroups?') || path == '/asc/v1/betaGroups')` — and never inspects the query string. A regression back to `page[limit]` would pass all 29 IT tests (they just did, on any params) and only surface as a red 07:17Z cron run, which is precisely how this bug shipped.

Suggest asserting the query in the fake handler, e.g.:

```dart
final q = req.uri.queryParameters;
if (q['limit'] != '200' || q.keys.any((k) => k.startsWith('page['))) {
  return serveJson(req, {"errors": [{"status": "400", "code": "PARAMETER_ERROR.ILLEGAL"}]}, 400);
}
```

That turns this fix into a regression-tested contract instead of relying on tomorrow's daily run as "the live proof" (the PR's own words).
"}

{"path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment2.md","content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: the 160-char error truncation hid the offending parameter**

`raw[0, 160]` cut the ASC 400 body right at `"title" : "A given param…` — before the named parameter — which is why this fix had to be diagnosed "by elimination + docs + fastlane" (the PR description says so itself). ASC error bodies are JSON: `{"errors":[{"id":…,"status":"400","code":"PARAMETER_ERROR.ILLEGAL","detail":…}]}` — consider appending a compact extraction of the parsed error fields (code + detail/title, e.g. `parsed["errors"]&.first&.slice("code", "detail")`) or simply raising the cap, so future 400s name the guilty parameter directly. The same pattern is repeated on the group-builds and `/v1/builds` raises below.
"}

